1
00:00:00,340 --> 00:00:03,669
The pilot author has a ticket for the
order validator.

2
00:00:03,849 --> 00:00:08,631
The assistant needs to understand two
files and propose a small edit with a

3
00:00:08,631 --> 00:00:09,651
regression test.

4
00:00:09,831 --> 00:00:14,417
The manager and platform lead agree on
that task before choosing permissions.

5
00:00:14,597 --> 00:00:19,419
In the same team, remote assistants can
have different network and credential

6
00:00:19,419 --> 00:00:23,929
access, so we make this decision for this
particular tool and workspace.

7
00:00:24,109 --> 00:00:26,693
The answer to who may do what is written
down.

8
00:00:28,173 --> 00:00:29,723
First map the data.

9
00:00:29,903 --> 00:00:33,307
The editor reads approved repository
context.

10
00:00:33,487 --> 00:00:37,647
The model request goes to the approved
service for that configuration.

11
00:00:37,827 --> 00:00:43,223
A remote workspace is another place to run
a client, and the provider, endpoint, and

12
00:00:43,223 --> 00:00:46,242
retention settings need their own
confirmation.

13
00:00:46,422 --> 00:00:51,471
The manager asks the platform owner to
record these details, along with which

14
00:00:51,471 --> 00:00:53,962
code and test data this pilot permits.

15
00:00:55,423 --> 00:01:00,320
Allow the agent to read the needed
repository context and propose edits on

16
00:01:00,320 --> 00:01:01,577
the working branch.

17
00:01:01,757 --> 00:01:06,470
Keep test execution within the approved
environment and choose the permissions

18
00:01:06,470 --> 00:01:07,920
supported by that setup.

19
00:01:08,100 --> 00:01:12,914
The author confirms the resulting diff and
publishes the branch with their own

20
00:01:12,914 --> 00:01:13,902
approved access.

21
00:01:14,082 --> 00:01:17,688
The reviewer has a separate identity and
submits the review.

22
00:01:17,868 --> 00:01:20,898
Production access is outside the pilot
scope.

23
00:01:22,340 --> 00:01:27,192
Now imagine the assistant thinks it needs
a new dependency to write the test.

24
00:01:27,372 --> 00:01:30,446
The author sees a request to make a
network call.

25
00:01:30,626 --> 00:01:35,540
The planned test uses the standard
library, so the author declines the action

26
00:01:35,540 --> 00:01:38,603
and asks for a solution within the agreed
files.

27
00:01:38,783 --> 00:01:41,079
Pause here and decide what you would do.

28
00:01:41,259 --> 00:01:45,229
Write down how the team reports repeated
unexpected requests.

29
00:01:46,673 --> 00:01:51,111
The author checks the diff, tests the
change, and opens the pull request.

30
00:01:51,291 --> 00:01:55,886
From an approved remote terminal, a
separate reviewer sees the stated scope,

31
00:01:55,886 --> 00:01:58,183
the actual diff, and the check result.

32
00:01:58,363 --> 00:02:00,606
They can approve or ask for a correction.

33
00:02:00,786 --> 00:02:05,446
A manager can ask for the pull request
link and review record as evidence that

34
00:02:05,446 --> 00:02:09,031
those responsibilities stayed separate
through the workflow.

35
00:02:10,507 --> 00:02:15,341
The manager records the approved
environment, data scope, action limits,

36
00:02:15,341 --> 00:02:19,167
named human roles, and the person who
handles exceptions.

37
00:02:19,347 --> 00:02:24,882
Apply the same questions if you later
pilot Claude Code or OpenCode inside the

38
00:02:24,882 --> 00:02:26,727
corporate cloud workspace.

39
00:02:26,907 --> 00:02:32,484
Ask which identity actually grants access
and which controls the workspace enforces.

40
00:02:32,664 --> 00:02:37,394
A new connector, new data source, or
expanded permission brings the decision

41
00:02:37,394 --> 00:02:39,075
back to the platform owner.
